<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jerusalem, Jerusalem (Chaim Topol, The Ed Sullivan Show)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Spanish version (Yafa Yarkoni)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yiddish version (David Eshet)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Finnish version by Sauvo Puhtila (Carola Standertskjöld)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jerusalem of Gold (Daliah Lavi, London Festival Orchestra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1969</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pour Sacha (film)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Schindler's List (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1993</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in (the Pour Sacha recording)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hoist (Phish)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1994</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pa'amonei HaYovel (Ofra Haza)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performance</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mon Pays C'est La Terre (Hélène Ségara)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">French translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Portuguese version (Roberto Carlos)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Blue Stars Drum and Bugle Corps, La Crosse, Wisconsin</span><span class="sg-chain-h">corps song</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pello Joxepe (Basque lullaby)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of most of the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Talmudic legend of Rabbi Akiva</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the title's jewel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Zion ha-lo Tish'ali (Yehuda Halevi, Songs to Zion)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">alluded to</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Book of Lamentations</span><span class="sg-chain-h">phrases drawn from</span></li><li><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a><span class="sg-chain-h">line drawn from</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Isaiah 6:7</span><span class="sg-chain-h">alluded to</span></li></ul></section>
